            <ArticleTitle>Severe medication-induced peripheral neuropathy treated with topical doxepin cream in a paediatric patient with leukaemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-219900</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-219900</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 17-year-old female with recently relapsed acute lymphoblastic leukaemia and a treatment course complicated by rhinocerebral mucormycosis infection developed severe peripheral neuropathy during the treatment for mucormycosis infection. This was felt to be a medication side effect. Her peripheral neuropathy was refractory to many well-established treatments, but ultimately responded dramatically and consistently to a novel therapy, topical doxepin cream (5%). This case report is the first published report of the application of topical doxepin cream for treatment of peripheral neuropathy in a paediatric patient.</AbstractText>
